<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">weather</ansi>

Outdoor zones have their own weather: clear skies, overcast, rain,
storms, and fog, each with its own odds. It changes on its own, at
random intervals, without anyone doing anything.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">The </ansi><ansi fg="command">weather</ansi><ansi fg="yellow-bold"> command</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">weather</ansi>
  Shows the time of day, the air temperature here if it can be judged,
  and:

  - <ansi fg="yellow">Sky:</ansi> how cloudy it is, and a line describing the current
    condition (rain, fog, and so on).
  - Whether fog is thickening around you.
  - At night, the moon's phase, if clouds aren't hiding it.

  Indoors with no view outside, you're simply told you can't see the
  sky. Indoors near an exit that looks outside, you get a glimpse of
  the weather through it instead.

  A zone with no weather table of its own (most settlements) has none
  to show.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How weather affects you</ansi>

Bad weather outdoors, in a tracked zone, does three things:

  - It slows a route: worse weather adds to how long a journey takes
    (<ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>). This is set once, when you set out.
  - It tires you faster: worse weather adds to the Hunger, Thirst, and
    Fatigue a route costs, and to the Fatigue a step of walking costs
    (<ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>).
  - It spoils rest: resting at a camp in foul weather recovers less
    Fatigue than the same rest under clear skies (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>).

Cold weather also chills the air, which can drain your Fatigue and,
if it's severe enough, hurt you, the same as any other cold exposure
(<ansi fg="command">help temperature</ansi>).

Fog thick enough to matter also makes things harder to see and hit in
the dark (<ansi fg="command">help light</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Forecasts</ansi>

A wizard in your company reads the sky: <ansi fg="command">weather</ansi> then also
says what comes next, and at higher levels when, and in the zones next
door. Forecasts are never wrong (<ansi fg="command">help forecast</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help forecast</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help temperature</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help strain</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help light</ansi>.
